    "topic_title": "Types of Chemical Bonds",
    "stem": "Hydrogen has an electronegativity of 2.2, and chlorine has an electronegativity of 3.2. Which description best characterizes the H–Cl bond and its electron distribution?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The bond is nonpolar covalent, with electrons shared equally because both atoms are nonmetals.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Nonmetal shortcut: Two nonmetals generally form a covalent bond, but that does not establish equal sharing. Their unequal electronegativities make the H–Cl bond polar. Fix: Compare the atoms’ electronegativities before deciding whether sharing is equal or unequal."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The bond is polar covalent, with H carrying a partial negative charge; the bond also has some ionic character.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed partial charges: This assigns greater electron density to the less electronegative atom. Cl attracts the shared electrons more strongly than H, so Cl—not H—carries the partial negative charge. Fix: Assign δ− to the atom with the higher electronegativity."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The bond is polar covalent, with Cl carrying a partial negative charge; the bond also has some ionic character.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Cl is more electronegative than H, so it attracts the shared electrons more strongly and carries a partial negative charge. This unequal sharing makes the bond polar covalent, and its charge separation gives it some ionic character."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The bond is ionic, with electrons completely transferred to Cl because the atoms have unequal electronegativities.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unequal-electronegativity means ionic: An electronegativity difference does not automatically imply complete electron transfer. In H–Cl, the electrons are shared unequally, producing a polar covalent bond rather than an ionic bond. Fix: Distinguish unequal sharing from electron transfer when classifying a bond."
     }
    ]
   },
